WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:06.670
I want to share with you a few toy simulations
that model how an epidemic spreads.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:11.980
There have recently been a few wonderful interactive
articles in this vein, including one in the

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:17.010
Washington Post by Harry Stevens, and one
by Kevin Similer over at Melting Asphalt;

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:20.420
they’re great, you can play with them, it’s
very informative, and of course I’ll leave

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.620
links in the description.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.919
After seeing those I had a few more questions.

00:00:24.919 --> 00:00:29.539
Like, if people stay away from each other
I get how that will slow the spread, but what

00:00:29.539 --> 00:00:33.710
if despite mostly staying away from each other
people still occasionally go to a central

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:37.210
location like a grocery store or a school?

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:41.060
Also, what if you are able to identify and
isolate the cases?

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:46.800
And if you can, what if a few slip through,
say because they show no symptoms and aren’t

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:47.800
tested?

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.800
How does travel between separate communities
affect things?

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.460
And what if people avoid contact with others
for a while, but then they kind of get tired

00:00:55.460 --> 00:01:01.609
of it and stop?

00:01:01.609 --> 00:01:10.990
We’ll explore these questions and more,
but first let’s walk through how exactly

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:12.630
our models will work.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:17.140
These simulations represent what’s called
an “SIR model”, meaning the population

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:22.070
is broken up into three categories, those
who are susceptible to the given disease,

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:26.630
those who are infectious, and those who have
recovered from the infection.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:34.210
The way I’ve written them, for every unit
of time a susceptible person spends within

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:39.150
a certain “infection radius” of someone
with the disease, they’ll have some probability

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.340
of contracting it themselves.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:45.640
So we’re using physical proximity as a stand-in
for things like shaking hands, touching the

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.960
same surfaces, kissing, sneezing on each other,
all that good stuff.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:54.220
Then for each infectious person, after a given
period of time, they’ll recover and no longer

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:56.540
be able to spread the disease.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:01.310
If they die, they also won’t be able to
spread it anymore, so as a more generic term

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:07.150
sometimes people consider the “R” in “SIR”
to stand for “removed”.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:12.170
This should go without saying, but I’ll
emphasize it anyway: These are toy models,

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:16.870
with a tiny population, inevitably falling
far short of the complexities in real people

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:18.250
and larger populations.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:23.349
I’m no epidemiologist, so I’d be very
hesitant to generalize any of the lessons

00:02:23.349 --> 00:02:26.250
here without deeper consideration.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:30.359
That said, I think it can be healthy to engage
the little scientist inside all of us and

00:02:30.359 --> 00:02:37.100
take the chance to be experimental and quantitative
even if it’s in a necessarily limited fashion,

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:40.349
especially if the alternative is to dwell
on headlines and uncertainty.

00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:44.609
Establishing simulations
We’ll start simple and layer on more complexity

00:02:44.609 --> 00:02:45.819
gradually.

00:02:45.819 --> 00:02:49.939
In these first runs that you’re seeing,
everyone just meanders about the city, and

00:02:49.939 --> 00:02:57.079
the infection follows the rules we’ve laid
out.

00:02:57.079 --> 00:03:02.799
So... it’s not looking great.

00:03:02.799 --> 00:03:09.409
It doesn’t take too long before almost everyone
gets infected.

00:03:09.409 --> 00:03:14.370
As a sanity check, what would you expect to
happen if I double this radius of infection?

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:18.310
You might think of this as representing more
total interactions between people; a more

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:20.269
socially engaged society.

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:25.980
It’ll spread more quickly, of course, but
how much?

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:31.260
It’s actually very dramatic!

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:37.209
Within a short span the majority of our little
population is infected simultaneously.

00:03:37.209 --> 00:03:41.640
As another sanity check, what do you expect
if we go back to the original infection radius,

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:45.739
and then cut the probability of infection
in half?

00:03:45.739 --> 00:03:50.329
Remember, for each unit of time a susceptible
person is within the infection radius of an

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:55.419
infectious person, they have some probability
of becoming infected, which by default I have

00:03:55.419 --> 00:03:56.579
set to 20%.

00:03:56.579 --> 00:03:58.719
This is the number we’re cutting in half
now.

00:03:58.719 --> 00:04:16.810
You could think of this better hand washing,
cough protection, and less face touching.

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:19.370
As you might expect, it spreads out the curve.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:24.130
In fact, it does so by quite a lot, really
illustrating how changes to hygiene can have

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:30.430
very large effects on the rate of spreading.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:35.950
The first key takeaway to tuck away in your
mind is just how sensitive this growth is

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:38.200
to each parameter in our control.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:42.590
It’s not hard to imagine changing your daily
habits in ways that multiply the number of

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:47.380
people you interact with or that cut your
probability of catching an infection in half;

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:51.820
but the implications for the pace of this
spread are huge.

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:56.660
The goal is probably to reduce the total number
of people who die, which is some proportion

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:59.660
of this “removed” category in the end.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:01.880
That proportion is not a constant, though.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:05.880
If you get to a point where the peak of this
infection curve is too high, meaning there’s

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.720
a time when many people are sick at once,
that’s when available healthcare resources

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.010
are overwhelmed, increasing that mortality
rate.

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:19.680
Now, I don’t know where you’re from, but
in most towns, people don’t actually spend

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.290
their days drunkenly wandering through the
city like this.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:26.000
Often there’s a common destination, like
a central market or a school, that people

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.000
need to go to.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.970
In our model, if we introduce a central spot
like this that people regularly visit then

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:35.970
return from, it’s...well...just look.

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:42.850
One of the main things I was curious about
is how to mitigate this effect.

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:45.560
We’ll examine that in just a bit.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:50.480
Another feature we could include is to have
a few separate communities, with travel between

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:51.480
them.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:56.940
Every day, each person will have some probability
of traveling to another community, where they

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:00.060
arrive at a central location.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:07.180
That’s our basic setup, now what actions
help to stop this spread?

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:14.040
What’s most effective is to identify and
isolate whoever is infectious, for example

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:16.070
with good testing and quick responsiveness.

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:21.210
For our simulations, once we hit some critical
threshold of cases, we’ll start sending

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:25.770
people to a separate location 1 day after
they get the infection.

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:30.010
This is, of course, just a stand-in for whatever
isolation would look like in the real world;

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:34.700
it doesn’t have to literally be transporting
all the sick people in one sad box.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:39.340
Unsurprisingly, this totally halts an epidemic
in its tracks.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:43.971
But what if when you’re infected, you have
a 20% chance of not getting quarantined, say

00:06:43.971 --> 00:06:48.570
because you show no symptoms and don’t get
tested, so you go about your day as usual?

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:53.530
We’ll illustrate these people who have no
symptoms with yellow circles instead of red.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:58.690
Obviously this will have a result somewhere
between the total quarantine and doing nothing,

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:18.400
but where on that spectrum would you predict
it will be?

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:23.430
The peak number of cases at the same time
is only a tiny bit higher, but there’s a

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:28.280
long tail as it takes much longer to stamp
out, resulting in about twice as many total

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:31.050
cases.

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.560
This gets more interesting when we do it in
the setting with many communities and travel

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:35.940
between them.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:40.610
Again, totally effective tracking and isolation
stops the epidemic very effectively, but what

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:46.630
do you predict will happen if 20% of infectious
cases slip through this process?

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:50.960
Again, I’ve set things to wait until a certain
critical threshold of cases is hit before

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:56.240
our little dot society kicks into gear and
takes action.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:58.592
Even when all the parameters are the same,
it’s interesting that in some runs it can

00:07:58.592 --> 00:08:02.170
take around three times longer to reach this
point than others; before the law of large

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:06.240
numbers kicks in, the pace of growth can have
as much to do with a role of the dice as it

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:21.610
does with anything intrinsic to the disease
itself.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:24.770
It does keep the curve flat, but now it’s
a much thicker tail during the long time it

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:35.659
takes to track all the cases, with over half
the population getting affected this time.

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:41.759
What would you predict if only 50% of infectious
cases are isolated like this?

00:08:41.759 --> 00:08:45.630
If half the infectious population is getting
quarantined, it doesn’t mean half the total

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:48.240
population will end up with the disease.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:52.519
Because there are still so many agents out
there spreading it, we end up with a situation

00:08:52.519 --> 00:09:00.710
that’s only barely better than if nothing
had been done at all.

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:05.370
A second key takeaway here is that changes
in how many people slip through the tests

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:12.110
cause disproportionately large changes to
the total number of people infected.

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:17.870
The fact that quickly containing cases early
is so effective has an interesting corollary,

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:23.040
which is that the most lethal diseases are,
in some sense, less dangerous globally.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:26.920
Remember that the rule in these quarantine
simulations is to send infectious people to

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:30.140
a separate location one day after they’ve
been infected.

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:35.730
But if the disease kills its host in one day,
the model looks identical, it just has a much

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:37.170
darker interpretation.

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:42.550
It’s terrible for those who get it, but
it doesn’t spread.

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:47.449
It also means that the most dangerous viruses
are the ones that kill some part of the population,

00:09:47.449 --> 00:09:50.629
while laying unnoticed and spreadable among
others.

00:09:50.629 --> 00:09:57.320
Or worse yet, if they remain unnoticed and
spreadable in everyone before becoming lethal.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:02.820
One of the reasons the SARS outbreak in 2002
was so well contained is that just about everyone

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:07.660
in this infectious population was showing
symptoms, and so were easier to identify and

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:08.769
isolate.

00:10:08.769 --> 00:10:15.339
A more optimistic corollary is how useful
early treatment can be.

00:10:15.339 --> 00:10:19.949
If there exists an antiviral therapeutic that
can move people out of this infectious category

00:10:19.949 --> 00:10:24.830
quickly, it has the same effect on the model
as isolating those cases.

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:33.550
But let’s say you don’t have widespread
testing, or antiviral therapeutics.

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:38.209
We’ll introduce a new parameter here, which
is how much people try to avoid each other.

00:10:38.209 --> 00:10:41.059
Let’s call it the “social distance factor”.

00:10:41.059 --> 00:10:45.520
In these animations, I’ll apply it as a
repulsive force between people, and have them

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:49.899
glow yellow when they feel a little too close
to their neighbor.

00:10:49.899 --> 00:10:54.639
This has the sad-but-cute effect that when
our little agents are social distancing, they

00:10:54.639 --> 00:10:57.689
often end up trembling near the edge.

00:10:57.689 --> 00:11:01.360
No isolation is perfect, though, so every
now and then even those

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:07.329
repulsed by others will jiggle close enough
to get infected; the point is that it’s

00:11:07.329 --> 00:11:08.550
much rarer.

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:10.990
Let’s take a look at four separate runs
here.

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:15.249
In each of them, after they hit 50 cases,
we’ll turn on social distancing.

00:11:15.249 --> 00:11:21.509
For the top left, we turn it on for everyone,
in the top right, we turn it on for 90% of

00:11:21.509 --> 00:11:22.839
the population.

00:11:22.839 --> 00:11:29.350
In the bottom left, 70%, and in the bottom
right, 50%.

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:38.439
What do you think will happen?

00:11:38.439 --> 00:11:43.079
As you might expect, when 100% of people avoid
each other, the disease quickly goes away

00:11:43.079 --> 00:11:45.009
entirely.

00:11:45.009 --> 00:11:50.470
In all four cases, the presence of widespread
social distancing absolutely flattens the

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:51.470
curve.

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:56.069
However, in terms of the long-term number
of cases, the run with 70%, and even the one

00:11:56.069 --> 00:12:01.149
with 90%, end up with a little less than half
the population ultimately getting infected,

00:12:01.149 --> 00:12:03.480
and the run with 50% is only a little bit
worse than that.

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:10.470
The case with 90% of people repelled by all
others takes longer to get there, but evidently

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:16.660
a mere 10% of the population cheating adds
enough instability to keep the fire slowly

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:19.180
burning for a long time.

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:26.800
Again, I’ll emphasize these are toy models,
and I leave it to the intelligence of the

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:31.720
viewer to judge if the behavior of these dots
accurately reflects what social distancing

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:34.600
would mean for you and your life.

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:38.720
Someone fully sequestered to their home is
not necessarily affected by the random jigglings

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:40.079
of their neighbor.

00:12:40.079 --> 00:12:45.180
But then again, few people live genuinely
independently from everyone else.

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:50.029
Insofar as these models aren’t outlandish,
the third key takeaway is that social distancing

00:12:50.029 --> 00:12:54.389
absolutely works to flatten the curve, but
even small imperfections prolong the spread

00:12:54.389 --> 00:12:56.509
for a while.

00:12:56.509 --> 00:13:02.720
Now let’s look at our setup with 12 communities
and travel between them.

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:07.790
By default, I have it set where every day
each agent has a 2% chance of traveling to

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:10.759
the center of a different community.

00:13:10.759 --> 00:13:17.290
Let’s try a run where once we hit 100 cases,
we cut down this travel rate by a factor of

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:19.509
4, to only half a percent.

00:13:19.509 --> 00:13:36.509
What do you think will happen?

00:13:36.509 --> 00:13:39.309
The honest answer is that it depends.

00:13:39.309 --> 00:13:44.869
In some runs, it doesn’t make any difference
and the majority of every community gets it.

00:13:44.869 --> 00:13:49.110
Other times, a few communities end up unscathed.

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:57.259
In general, the earlier you turn this on the
more effective it is, but the takeaway here

00:13:57.259 --> 00:14:02.300
is that reducing contact between communities
has only a limited effect once many communities

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:09.800
already have it.

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:13.880
As a side note, when we run these simulations
with larger cities, which has the effect that

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:19.509
the city centers act like concentrated urban
hubs, you can see how once the infection hits

00:14:19.509 --> 00:14:30.429
one urban center, it very quickly hits all
of them, and slowly spreads to the edges from

00:14:30.429 --> 00:14:32.899
there.

00:14:32.899 --> 00:14:38.870
Let’s take a moment to talk about how to
quantify the rate of this spread.

00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:42.850
Consider one person with the disease, and
count how many other people they infect while

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:46.160
they have it.

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:50.369
The average for this count across everyone
who has been sick is known as the effective

00:14:50.369 --> 00:14:53.309
reproductive number, or R.

00:14:53.309 --> 00:14:59.720
A more commonly discussed number is R_0, which
is the value of R in a fully susceptible population,

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:01.350
like at the very beginning.

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:06.819
This is called the “Basic” reproductive
number.

00:15:06.819 --> 00:15:11.329
You may notice I’ve had this label on the
simulations, where the way it’s calculated

00:15:11.329 --> 00:15:16.339
is to look at each individual who is currently
infectious, count how many they’ve infected

00:15:16.339 --> 00:15:21.860
so far, estimate how many they’ll infect
in total based on the duration of the illness,

00:15:21.860 --> 00:15:25.959
then average those numbers.

00:15:25.959 --> 00:15:31.329
For example, in our first default simulation
with no added spices, R is around 2.2 in the

00:15:31.329 --> 00:15:36.009
highest growth phase, before falling down
as the population becomes saturated.

00:15:36.009 --> 00:15:41.720
By contrast, when we doubled the infection
radius, R was as high as 8!

00:15:41.720 --> 00:15:46.800
That factor has a huge effect on the growth
rate, hence the explosive spreading.

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:51.559
This kind of makes sense; when you double
that radius, there’s about 4 times as many

00:15:51.559 --> 00:15:54.329
people inside it to infect.

00:15:54.329 --> 00:16:02.139
When we chopped the infection rate in half,
it hovered around the 1.3 to 1.7 range.

00:16:02.139 --> 00:16:07.600
While R is greater than 1, the infection is
growing exponentially, and is known as an

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:08.600
epidemic.

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:13.329
If it holds steady around 1, it’s called
“endemic”.

00:16:13.329 --> 00:16:16.610
And less than 1 means it’s on the decline.

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:22.629
For comparison, R0 for COVID-19 is estimated
to be a little above 2, which is also around

00:16:22.629 --> 00:16:27.339
the mean estimate for R0 during the 1918 Spanish
flu pandemic.

00:16:27.339 --> 00:16:30.839
The seasonal flu, by comparison, is much lower,
around 1.3.

00:16:30.839 --> 00:16:36.420
For our toy epidemic in the travel case, when
we turn on social distancing and shut down

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:53.360
travel, you can see R quickly drop down from
2.

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:56.989
Like I said at the start, one of the things
I was most curious about is the effects of

00:16:56.989 --> 00:17:01.089
some kind of shared central location, like
a market or a school.

00:17:01.089 --> 00:17:08.900
When I introduce such a destination for our
little dots, R jumps up as high as 5.8.

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:13.390
This might be a little unfair, since it puts
everyone right in the same spot, and given

00:17:13.390 --> 00:17:17.390
that we’re using physical proximity as a
stand in for things like shaking hands or

00:17:17.390 --> 00:17:21.730
kissing, we should acknowledge that people
going to the same school or grocery store

00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:27.600
are not as likely to spread an infection as,
say, people living in the same house.

00:17:27.600 --> 00:17:33.059
To account for this let’s cut the probability
of infection per day in half.

00:17:33.059 --> 00:17:38.110
This does indeed cut the peak value of R in
half, but the effect of a central market remains

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:42.450
dramatic.

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:51.131
Let’s try a run where after some threshold
is hit, we turn on social distancing, but

00:17:51.131 --> 00:17:59.929
people still go to the central location as
usual.

00:17:59.929 --> 00:18:04.950
You may have noticed that some of the dots
have escaped their little cage...which was

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:09.669
not supposed to happen, but I’m gonna make
the conscious choice not to fix that.

00:18:09.669 --> 00:18:13.640
It’s like they all looked at the chaos inside
and went “Nope, I’m out!

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:15.570
I don’t want any part of that!”.

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:19.880
Living in the Bay area during a shelter-in-place
order, I can confirm this is how some people

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:21.440
react.

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:26.900
Wandering dots aside, though, let me show
you how this graph compares to the control,

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:30.789
and how it compares to what would have happened
if in addition to repelling away from each

00:18:30.789 --> 00:18:35.460
other, all the dots also stopped going to
the central location.

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:40.039
The peak of the infection curve is a bit lower
than the control, but in terms of total number

00:18:40.039 --> 00:18:48.140
of cases, keeping that central location active
really defeats the effects of social distancing.

00:18:48.140 --> 00:18:55.649
Now what do you think will be more effective,
if on top of the social distancing we decrease

00:18:55.649 --> 00:19:00.750
the frequency with which people go to the
central spot by a factor of 5?

00:19:00.750 --> 00:19:06.620
Or if we chop the probability of infection
down by another factor of 2, for example meaning

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:08.890
people practice better hygiene?

00:19:08.890 --> 00:19:12.820
Well, let’s play them both out.

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:18.220
The left here requires our dots to heavily
alter their daily routines, whereas the right

00:19:18.220 --> 00:19:23.659
involves people continuing their usual habits
while being much more conscious of hygiene.

00:19:23.659 --> 00:19:28.940
They’re actually nearly identical, which
surprised me, given that one of them is a

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:31.760
fivefold increase and the other is twofold.

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:36.799
I guess it goes to show that more effective
hygiene, which is maybe easier said than done,

00:19:36.799 --> 00:19:39.799
goes a long way.

00:19:39.799 --> 00:19:44.350
Of course, it doesn’t have to be an either
or; our goal with these experiments is to

00:19:44.350 --> 00:19:46.940
look at the effects of one change at a time.

00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:51.140
If you’re curious, here’s what it looks
like where we apply social distancing, restrict

00:19:51.140 --> 00:19:56.870
the rate that people go to the central location,
and also lower the infection rate, all at

00:19:56.870 --> 00:19:58.470
once.

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:03.299
The combination of them all is, indeed, very
effective.

00:20:03.299 --> 00:20:07.600
But I want to emphasize again how the most
desirable case is one where you can consistently

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:10.330
identify and isolate cases.

00:20:10.330 --> 00:20:15.320
Even in this central market simulation, which
left unchecked gives a huge conflagration,

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:19.470
being able to do this effectively really halts
the epidemic.

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:23.080
And our little dots don’t even have to be
repelled by each other or stop their trips

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:25.450
to the market.

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:31.880
In real epidemiology it gets more sophisticated
than this, with tactics like “contact tracing”

00:20:31.880 --> 00:20:36.130
where you not only identify and isolate known
cases, but you do the same for everyone who’s

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:42.610
been in contact with those cases.

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:46.500
Given the time I’m posting this, I imagine
there’s some expectation for it to be a

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:48.090
PSA on social distancing.

00:20:48.090 --> 00:20:51.889
But to be honest, that’s not really my own
main takeaway.

00:20:51.889 --> 00:20:57.020
To be clear, when it’s needed, like it is
now, social distancing absolutely saves lives,

00:20:57.020 --> 00:21:02.020
and as we saw earlier when people cheat, or
continue regularly meeting in a central spot,

00:21:02.020 --> 00:21:06.020
it has disproportionate effects on the long-term
number of cases.

00:21:06.020 --> 00:21:10.990
The uncomfortable truth, though, is that while
the disease still exists, as soon as people

00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:14.799
let up and go back to their normal lives,
if there’s nothing in place to contain the

00:21:14.799 --> 00:21:22.290
cases you’ll just get a second wave.

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:26.929
After making all these, what I came away with
more than anything was a deeper appreciation

00:21:26.929 --> 00:21:32.070
for disease control done right; for the inordinate
value of early widespread testing and the

00:21:32.070 --> 00:21:37.370
ability to isolate cases; for the therapeutics
that treat these cases, and most importantly

00:21:37.370 --> 00:21:41.539
for how easy it is to underestimate all that
value when times are good.

00:21:41.539 --> 00:21:46.600
I’m writing this during a pandemic, when
some viewers may be able to identify all too

00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:50.510
well with the trembling dots retreating to
the edge of their box.

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:55.121
But in the future many people will be watching
it during a pandemic that never was; a time

00:21:55.121 --> 00:22:00.230
when a novel pathogen that would spread widely
if left unchecked is instead swiftly found

00:22:00.230 --> 00:22:02.090
and contained.

00:22:02.090 --> 00:22:06.350
Those would-be pandemics never make it into
the history books, which is maybe why we don’t

00:22:06.350 --> 00:22:09.750
value the heros behind them the way we should.

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:13.409
Living in a world with widespread travel and
vibrant urban centers does make fighting the

00:22:13.409 --> 00:22:17.200
spread of a disease an uphill battle, it’s
true.

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:21.799
But that same level of connectedness means
ideas spread more quickly than ever, ideas

00:22:21.799 --> 00:22:25.620
that can lead to the systems and technologies
which nip outbreaks in the bud.

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:30.059
It won’t happen on it’s own, and it’s
clear that we sometimes make mistakes, but

00:22:30.059 --> 00:22:38.669
I’m fundamentally optimistic about our ability
to learn from those mistakes.

00:22:38.669 --> 00:22:41.950
As you might imagine, these videos require
a lot of hours and effort.

00:22:41.950 --> 00:22:46.610
I don’t do ad reads at the end, and YouTube
content related to the current pandemic seems

00:22:46.610 --> 00:22:50.990
to be systematically demonetized, so I just
wanted to take this chance to say a particularly

00:22:50.990 --> 00:22:53.740
warm thanks to those who support them directly
on Patreon.

00:22:53.740 --> 00:22:55.450
It really does make a difference.

